Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used a national UNOS cohort of ACHD heart-transplant recipients across the United States, which reduces single-center selection bias, although subgroup size remained modest.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: The model included recipient, donor, procedural, and post-transplant variables; this improves prediction but mixes pre- and post-transplant information, which may limit use as a strictly pre-transplant decision tool.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcome was clearly defined as mortality at 1 year and 3 years after heart transplantation.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include internal validation, oversampling, SHAP-based feature selection, and explainability. Uncertainty remains because external validation and calibration assessment were not reported in the retrieved text.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly addresses AI-based outcome prediction after heart transplantation in a clinically important ACHD subgroup and is clearly relevant to the review question.  ￼

